# logd — the system log sink (real-logd).
#
# svcmgr launches and supervises logd post-handover, minting its bootstrap
# round from the reserved log-sink sources init endows: the master log
# endpoint RECV, a first-launch SEND for the HANDOVER_PULL of init-logd's
# boot history, a DEATH_EQ_AUTHORITY SEND|GRANT on procmgr (for sender slot
# reclaim), and the devmgr.class.serial and devmgr.class.framebuffer class
# SENDs (logd resolves the serial and framebuffer drivers via QUERY_DEVICE
# and routes output through them — it holds no UART hardware).
# `log_sink = yes` selects this svcmgr-minted round in place of seed /
# provides.
#
# `restart = on_failure`: svcmgr holds the master-log source for the system's
# life, so a fresh logd re-attaches to the same endpoint object every log
# sender already targets, skipping the one-shot history pull (no init-logd
# remains once an instance has taken all its bootstrap rounds; a restart
# before that is served the pull) and serving a fresh table; svcmgr writes
# the death's lines only once it runs, but for a first launch's death before
# its bootstrap request (see services/svcmgr/docs/restart-protocol.md). `critical =
# yes`: a permanently-down logd leaves the system blind, so graceful shutdown
# is the terminal escape once the restart budget is spent.
#
# `argv = logd` names the process for procmgr's LIST_PROCESSES, through
# which a debugger, and svctest's logd_reserve and logd_restart phases, find
# it.
#
# `reserve = yes` makes logd's budget node a system-reserve node, which may
# take the free pages memmgr keeps in reserve. oomd, crashd and sessiond, the
# reserve services that end a memory-pressure episode, write their lines
# through logd with a synchronous call, so a service they call on that path
# is a reserve service too (services/svcmgr/docs/service-definitions.md
# § `reserve`): outside the reserve, a first touch of logd's heap while the
# free pool is at the reserve is declined and kills it, and svcmgr's one
# create of its next instance is refused while the pool stays there, which,
# logd being critical, shuts the system down. A reserve holder has a core
# role, which `log_sink` gives logd already.
binary    = /services/logd
argv      = logd
priority  = 18
restart   = on_failure
critical  = yes
namespace = none
log_sink  = yes
reserve   = yes
